<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 11 and below (fullBackupContent).

  allowBackup defaults to true, but the Room database was never named here, so what actually
  got preserved was left to chance. A user who reinstalled AccA lost every script they had
  written, with no export path to fall back on. Naming the database explicitly makes the
  intent unambiguous.

  Room keeps three files: the database plus its write-ahead log and shared memory. Only the
  main file is worth backing up; -wal and -shm are transient and restoring a stale pair
  alongside a newer database can corrupt it, so they are excluded.

  Do NOT treat this as the backup story on its own. Auto Backup needs a backup transport and a
  matching signing key, and it silently does nothing for a sideloaded debug build. Explicit
  Export/Import from the overflow menu is the reliable route; this is the safety net for users
  who never think to use it.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <!-- The database is excluded, matching data_extraction_rules.xml. scripts_table holds shell
         commands that run as root, and Android 11 and below cannot tell a cloud upload apart from
         a local device transfer, so including it here means uploading them. A privacy property
         should not depend on the OS version. The cost is real and stated plainly: on these phones
         a reinstall no longer restores profiles, schedules or scripts, and Export/Import from the
         overflow menu is the only route. Preferences carry no script bodies and still go. -->
    <exclude domain="database" path="." />
    <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
